WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.279
Film Courage: So I know you’ve produced
numerous movies, you’ve worked for other

00:00:03.279 --> 00:00:08.130
people but you’ve wanted to direct for a
very long time and I was wondering what that

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.349
journey was like?

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:15.500
And now to finally be able to direct three
movies (and more are on the horizon it sounds

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.500
like), as well?

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.050
Passionflix, Co-Founder Tosca Musk: Well,
I did two movies prior to Passionflix.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.430
I directed two movies prior to Passionflix,
three movies actually.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.570
I started out as an actor and then went into
directing and then after directing my first

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.510
feature I realized it’s like really, really
hard to actually get it out and do things

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:36.500
and then I went into producing and I produced
for the next 10 years.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:43.890
It was after I had my kids that I realized
I wanted to do something that’s more creatively

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.620
fulfilling for me.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:52.149
And I went and met with an executive producer
who I produced a couple of movies for before

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:54.969
and she is an awesome woman.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:59.179
She works at Lifetime now (her name is Sharon
Bodis).

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:00.199
And she said “What do you want to do?”

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:01.280
And I said “I want to direct.”

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.659
And she goes “Hmmm…I’ll let you direct.

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:04.970
I have a movie now.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.390
Do you want to direct it?”

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.960
And I went “Yeah, sure!”

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:11.960
And she said “Okay.”

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.540
And within a month I was on prep with that
movie and directing.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.550
It was called YOU CAST A SPELL ON ME and that’s
the one the Joany saw.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:28.670
So having that lunch with her basically changed
my life and brought us to Passionflix because

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.220
she said I could direct that movie, Joany
saw it, she loved it, Joany contacted me and

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.620
now we have Passionflix.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.180
That’s basically it.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.590
That’s the short story.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.110
I got to make two movies for Sharon.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.500
I made that one and then the following year
they hired me to make a CINDERELLA CHRISTMAS,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.170
which was a Christmas story and also a love
story for ION as well.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.880
Great experience, that’s where I met Emma
Rigby and as soon as we made Passionflix,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.010
I asked Emma Rigby to be my first female lead
of Passionflix for HOLLYWOOD DIRT.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.740
So it all just sort of worked together.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.740
I was very fortunate.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:09.470
Of course it was 15 years of slogging to get
to that point.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.659
But I was very fortunate to be given that
opportunity by a great supporter of women.

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:18.849
Film Courage: So to know that someone believed
in you that quickly, where you were probably

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:21.980
just coming down the line and [possibly] this
was something (probably you weren’t expecting

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.840
it so fast), what was that first day on set
like?

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.959
Terrifying?

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:28.069
Empowering?

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:29.189
Both?

00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:34.680
Tosca Musk: It was exciting, actually.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.249
I definitely felt that I was in my own and
that I owned it for sure.

00:02:40.249 --> 00:02:42.040
It was a very positive experience.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.700
That entire movie was wonderful.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.689
Again the two lead actors that I worked with
(Ryan McPartlin and Nikki Deloach) had great

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:50.920
chemistry.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.050
We had great chemistry together.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.980
And so we still are in contact and we still
want to make another movie another.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.470
And so it was a really positive experience.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.420
I knew exactly what I wanted as a filmmaker.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:12.409
I had a great DP with me and he was very knowledgeable
(a lot of experience).

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:13.409
Oh!

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:16.420
Actually on that one I didn’t [have him]
but I had a great DP.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:21.629
She was new to the field in that particular
one but she was so supportive.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:28.540
So I had the DP that I had a lot of experience
on my next movie CINDERELLA CHRISTMAS.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:34.379
So it was a positive experience, it was just
fun.

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:38.340
And I think once you’re doing something
that you are passionate about and that you

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.560
really, truly believe you should be doing,
you do a great job, you excel in that.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.409
And that’s why to me it’s not considered
work, it’s considered play.

00:03:48.409 --> 00:03:52.420
So I can do it forever, which is great, I
have a great time.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.359
Film Courage: So going back to that first
experience [as a director], it was also the

00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:58.120
chemistry and the environment.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.310
It all seemed to kind of work?

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.209
Tosca Musk: Yeah, the chemistry and the environment,
I mean I think at the end of the day (and

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:08.060
I’ve been fortunate to have been told this
by a lot of my crew members) is that the environment

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.840
is created by the director.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.050
So the director, the mood on set is going
to be based on how the director is feeling

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.050
that day.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:20.030
And if I was throwing tantrums and yelling
at everybody, probably it wouldn’t have

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.380
been a great set to work on.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:32.419
But I arrived and we were laughing and joking
and having a fun time from the second we started

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:39.310
and so everybody was laughing and joking and
having a great time and that really helped.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:46.530
My producer Jina (who is the producer on all
the movies, as well) she and I have a No A**hole

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:48.120
Policy on the set.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.259
We actually give people a warning so when
we hire them we say “Hi, listen the only

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:55.210
drama that happens on set is going to be on
screen.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:00.430
So if you’re going to be dramatic in anyway
and cause problems, you are welcome to leave.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.639
You don’t have to stay.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:03.320
Nobody is making you work here.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.390
If you don’t like it, you are absolutely
welcome to leave.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.629
But if you do like it, we’d love for you
to stay and let’s have a great time.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:14.460
We are going to make a movie and it’s not
rocket science, it’s not brain surgery and

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.139
people’s lives are not on the line here.

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:19.139
So let’s just have fun creating something.”

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:22.979
And so that goes over really well with a lot
of crew members.

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:27.860
And there have been a few times that we’ve
had crew members that have been…(and actors)

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.590
people who have not been nice.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.220
And so we kindly invite them to leave…rarely.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:39.280
Film Courage: So having that sense of humor…yeah…I
would hope so…it happens…it definitely

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:40.280
does.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.699
Sometimes people clash, whatever.

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:43.870
I think also having a sense of humor [helps].

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.540
Sometimes I don’t always see that in people
and even if it’s just going to the grocery

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.509
story and it’s rare…maybe it’s just
because Los Angeles is so crowded and everybody

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:54.250
is thinking of being somewhere else, doing
something else.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:58.560
But maybe having that sense of humor and people
think “Okay, I can relax a little bit.”

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:06.169
Tosca Musk: Yeah, I’ve very funny on set
apparently!

00:06:06.169 --> 00:06:09.280
I love laughter.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.639
Romantic comedies are my strength so that
is what I love to make the most.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:17.960
HOLLYWOOD DIRT was definitely a great one
for me, it’s an empowering woman but it’s

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.520
a comedy and it’s a romance.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.840
And then MATCHMAKERS PLAYBOOK, it’s a romance
and a comedy.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.849
What was actually hard for me was AFTERBURN/AFTERSHOCK
because it’s a drama.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:35.349
So I had to try and figure out how to make
a drama and still keep it light and funny

00:06:35.349 --> 00:06:39.289
on set because there are so many dramatic
scenes in that movie.

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:43.539
But if you see the behind-the-scenes, as soon
as I call cut everyone is laughing and having

00:06:43.539 --> 00:06:44.539
a great time.

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:49.860
I whistle a lot so people…I whistle and
I sing on set, a lot!

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:55.169
Which is somewhat embarrassing because I don’t
realize that I’m doing it until the boom

00:06:55.169 --> 00:06:58.710
operator or the sound mixer will come up and
just start recording it and then they’ll

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.539
play it back and I’ll be like “Oh…wow!

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:02.819
Yes that’s me whistling on set.”

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:08.789
But it keeps it…there’s a levity then
to set, it just keeps everything light and

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:12.129
fun and I think that’s important.

00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:14.689
But romantic comedy is definitely my strength,
I say.

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:16.380
Although I did like making AFTERBURN/AFTERSHOCK.

